WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
In the alcohol reactions video, oxidising propan-two-ol gave

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.920
a ketone, propanone.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.680
Oxidise propan-one-ol and distil, as we did with

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.880
ethanol, and you get an aldehyde, propanal.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.920
Both have the formula C three H six

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:16.480
O.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.640
Both are colourless liquids.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.840
Yet warm each one with this reagent, and

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.160
one coats the tube in a silver mirror,

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.600
while the other does nothing.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.240
What's the difference, and how do we name

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.920
and test these compounds?

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:35.053
Both contain a carbonyl group, a carbon double

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:36.453
bonded to oxygen.

00:00:36.814 --> 00:00:39.933
Oxygen pulls the shared electrons more strongly, so

00:00:40.014 --> 00:00:42.573
the C double bond O is polar: the

00:00:42.654 --> 00:00:45.613
oxygen is slightly negative, and the carbon slightly

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:46.493
positive.

00:00:46.814 --> 00:00:48.933
The difference is where it sits.

00:00:49.214 --> 00:00:51.693
In an aldehyde, the carbonyl is at the

00:00:51.774 --> 00:00:54.093
end of the chain, so its carbon carries

00:00:54.134 --> 00:00:55.133
a hydrogen.

00:00:55.454 --> 00:00:58.093
In a ketone, it's inside the chain, with

00:00:58.134 --> 00:01:00.973
a carbon on each side, and no hydrogen.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.960
To name an aldehyde, find the longest chain

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.880
that includes the carbonyl carbon.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.240
Take the alkane's name, drop the final e,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.560
and add al.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.880
The carbonyl carbon is always carbon one, so

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.680
there's no number: methanal, ethanal, propanal, butanal.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.800
Any branch is numbered from the carbonyl end.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.920
So this one is two-methylpropanal.

00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:30.813
Ketones end in one.

00:01:31.094 --> 00:01:34.133
Propanone needs no number, because the carbonyl can

00:01:34.214 --> 00:01:35.813
only sit in the middle.

00:01:35.894 --> 00:01:38.213
Butanone is the same, though you'll often see

00:01:38.294 --> 00:01:40.293
it written butan-two-one.

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:43.413
From five carbons, the position matters.

00:01:43.494 --> 00:01:46.133
Number from the end nearer the carbonyl.

00:01:46.294 --> 00:01:50.293
This is pentan-two-one, and this is pentan-three-one.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.420
Now count the atoms in propanal and propanone.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.380
Both are C three H six O.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.460
Same formula, different functional group, so they're functional

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.340
group isomers.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.380
Once there are three or more carbons, every

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.180
aldehyde has a ketone isomer like this.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.820
When a question asks for every isomer with

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.300
a C double bond O, that pair is

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.420
easy to miss.

00:02:17.434 --> 00:02:20.233
Between the molecules, the polar C double bond

00:02:20.394 --> 00:02:24.713
O gives dipole-dipole attractions: the slightly positive carbon

00:02:24.794 --> 00:02:27.833
of one molecule attracts the slightly negative oxygen

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:28.713
of the next.

00:02:29.194 --> 00:02:31.433
That's on top of dispersion forces.

00:02:31.754 --> 00:02:34.393
But there's no O H, so these molecules

00:02:34.474 --> 00:02:36.313
can't hydrogen bond to each other.

00:02:36.874 --> 00:02:38.953
That puts their boiling points in the middle.

00:02:39.594 --> 00:02:42.713
For molecules of similar size, pentane boils at

00:02:42.794 --> 00:02:47.273
thirty six degrees, butanal at seventy five, butan-two-one

00:02:47.354 --> 00:02:50.953
at eighty, which is close, and butan-one-ol at

00:02:51.034 --> 00:02:52.473
one hundred and eighteen.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.180
And the aldehyde's hydrogen?

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.460
It's bonded to carbon, not oxygen, so it

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.100
isn't positive enough to hydrogen bond.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.460
But the carbonyl oxygen does have lone pairs.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.580
So water molecules can hydrogen bond to it.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.100
That's why small aldehydes and ketones, like propanone,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.460
mix with water.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.820
As the chain grows, solubility falls, just like

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:17.700
the alcohols.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:20.740
Now the chemistry.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.740
In the alcohol reactions video, a ketone stopped,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.300
because its carbonyl carbon had no hydrogen to

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:27.940
remove.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.340
An aldehyde still has one.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.020
So it's easily oxidised, even by mild oxidising

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:34.660
agents.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.420
That gives us tests.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.540
The aldehyde is oxidised, and the reagent is

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.220
reduced, which we can see.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:43.940
A ketone usually does nothing.

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:49.646
The first test is Tollens' reagent: silver ions,

00:03:49.807 --> 00:03:51.646
held in an ammonia solution.

00:03:51.967 --> 00:03:54.006
Warm it gently with an aldehyde in a

00:03:54.047 --> 00:03:55.086
water bath.

00:03:55.407 --> 00:03:58.126
The aldehyde reduces the silver ions to silver

00:03:58.287 --> 00:04:01.006
metal, which coats the glass as a mirror.

00:04:01.327 --> 00:04:04.086
With a ketone, the solution stays clear.

00:04:04.447 --> 00:04:07.726
Tollens' reagent is made fresh, just before use,

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:10.766
and disposed of straight after, because on standing

00:04:10.927 --> 00:04:13.086
it can form an explosive solid.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.300
The second is Fehling's solution, or the similar

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:19.580
Benedict's solution.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.540
It's deep blue, from copper two ions.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.860
Warm it with an aldehyde, and the copper

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.260
two ions are reduced to copper one, which

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.260
forms copper one oxide, a brick-red precipitate, meaning

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.100
a solid that forms in the solution.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.340
A ketone leaves it blue.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.980
So having a C double bond O isn't

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:38.540
enough.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.460
It takes that hydrogen.

00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:46.106
And acidified dichromate, from the alcohol reactions video?

00:04:46.387 --> 00:04:49.026
An aldehyde turns it green, and a ketone

00:04:49.107 --> 00:04:50.466
leaves it orange.

00:04:50.587 --> 00:04:53.186
So, for an aldehyde or a ketone, all

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:55.826
three tests ask one question.

00:04:55.947 --> 00:04:58.386
Is there a hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon?

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.240
Here's how this fits with oxidation.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.880
Oxidise an unknown alcohol.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.920
If the dichromate turns green, distil off the

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:08.800
product.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.640
Then test the product.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.200
A silver mirror means an aldehyde, so the

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:14.960
alcohol was primary.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.360
No mirror means a ketone, so it was

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:18.400
secondary.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.720
Don't rely on dichromate for this step.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.680
Primary and secondary alcohols turn it green too.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.567
So, aldehyde at the end, ketone in the

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:28.900
middle.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.660
Aldehydes end in al, ketones in one.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.740
Both have dipole-dipole forces, but no hydrogen bonds

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.380
between their own molecules.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.860
And every test asks one question: is there

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.780
an H on the carbonyl carbon?

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.580
Yes means an aldehyde: a silver mirror, a

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.860
brick-red solid, a green colour.

00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:52.506
Your turn.

00:05:52.587 --> 00:05:55.906
A compound has the condensed formula shown.

00:05:56.187 --> 00:05:58.906
State whether it's an aldehyde or a ketone,

00:05:58.947 --> 00:06:01.706
giving a reason, and give its IUPAC name.

00:06:02.187 --> 00:06:03.466
Pause here to answer.

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.946
The carbonyl carbon has a carbon on each

00:06:16.987 --> 00:06:18.986
side, so it's a ketone.

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:22.026
The longest chain has five carbons, with the

00:06:22.107 --> 00:06:24.746
carbonyl on carbon three from either end.

00:06:25.227 --> 00:06:26.986
So number to give the methyl the lower

00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:27.466
number.

00:06:27.707 --> 00:06:28.906
It's on carbon two.

00:06:29.307 --> 00:06:32.186
That's two-methylpentan-three-one.

00:06:32.307 --> 00:06:35.626
Four-methylpentan-three-one numbers from the wrong end.

00:06:37.634 --> 00:06:38.273
Last one.

00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:41.553
Compounds A and B both have the formula

00:06:41.794 --> 00:06:44.993
C four H eight O, and both contain

00:06:45.074 --> 00:06:46.753
a C double bond O group.

00:06:47.394 --> 00:06:50.353
Warmed with Tollens' reagent, A forms a silver

00:06:50.434 --> 00:06:52.833
mirror, and B shows no change.

00:06:53.474 --> 00:06:55.393
A has an unbranched chain.

00:06:55.954 --> 00:06:58.753
Identify A and B by name, and explain

00:06:58.794 --> 00:07:00.193
the difference in their results.

00:07:00.754 --> 00:07:02.033
Pause here to answer.

00:07:13.234 --> 00:07:16.353
A forms a mirror, so it was oxidised:

00:07:16.394 --> 00:07:18.833
an aldehyde, with an H on its carbonyl

00:07:18.914 --> 00:07:19.393
carbon.

00:07:19.874 --> 00:07:23.273
Unbranched, with four carbons, it's butanal.

00:07:23.794 --> 00:07:25.713
B has a C double bond O, but

00:07:25.874 --> 00:07:29.953
doesn't react, so it's a ketone: butan-two-one, with

00:07:30.194 --> 00:07:32.313
no H on its carbonyl carbon.

00:07:32.354 --> 00:07:34.593
Saying both should react because both have a

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:37.073
C double bond O misses the point.

00:07:37.154 --> 00:07:38.513
The hydrogen decides.

00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:43.333
So the carbonyl's position decides the name, the

00:07:43.494 --> 00:07:45.493
forces, and the test results.

00:07:46.054 --> 00:07:47.893
Next: into the lab.

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:52.133
Oxidising alcohols ourselves, collecting the products, and testing

00:07:52.254 --> 00:07:52.533
them.

00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:55.973
Here are the key notes.

00:07:56.214 --> 00:07:58.133
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:58.214 --> 00:07:59.013
to copy them.
